WEBVTT

1
00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:47.760
Okay, so my name is Theodora Etim

2
00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.080
from the Department of Theatre
and Media Studies

3
00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:53.720
at the University
of Calabar, Nigeria, and

4
00:14:54.400 --> 00:15:00.120
I will be speaking on the topic, “Digital
Nollywood as Interactive Narrative:

5
00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.800
Gender, Power, and Audience Rewriting.”

6
00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:07.280
Okay, straight to my presentation.

7
00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:10.160
So this presentation examines

8
00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:13.400
how audiences engage

9
00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:17.200
with digital Nollywood films
across online platforms.

10
00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:22.640
And it asks a simple question:
what happens when audiences do not simply

11
00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:28.000
consume stories, you know, but continue
writing them through digital interaction?

12
00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:33.240
Now, my argument
is that, audience comment

13
00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.800
sections on platforms

14
00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:39.840
such as YouTube and Instagram,

15
00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:45.000
they function as spaces of participatory
storytelling in a way

16
00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:49.800
where viewers actively shape,
negotiate, and extend

17
00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:53.000
narrative meaning.

18
00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:56.320
Okay, so

19
00:15:56.320 --> 00:16:00.120
electronic literature
scholarship has traditionally focused

20
00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:04.800
on born-digital text,
you know, such as hypertext fiction,

21
00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:09.080
digital poetry,
and experimental literary forms.

22
00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:10.280
While

23
00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:14.080
what I've mentioned remains important,
you see that less attention has been given

24
00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:19.120
to everyday forms of digital textuality
that emerges through audience interaction.

25
00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:23.080
So what my paper argues is that Nigerian
audience comment sections,

26
00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:28.200
they represent an overlooked, you know,
form of electronic literary practice.

27
00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:32.240
Rather than treating comments
as mere reactions to a film,

28
00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:36.400
I approach them as sites
where meaning is produced collectively.

29
00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:40.720
And in these spaces, audiences
interpret characters, debate

30
00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:43.120
moral choices,
they share personal experiences,

31
00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:46.480
and they contribute new layers
of meaning to the narrative.

32
00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:49.960
Now my core argument is this—

33
00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:53.080
is that

34
00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:56.560
narrative meaning is no longer fixed
at the point of production.

35
00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:58.320
You know, traditionally
we think of meaning

36
00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:02.360
as something created by the filmmaker
and received by the audience.

37
00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:06.400
However, you see that digital platforms,
they’ve complicated that relationship now.

38
00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:09.920
Audiences now participate
in what I describe as narrative extension.

39
00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:13.760
You know, they reinterpret every event,
they negotiate character

40
00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.000
motivations,
they challenge dominant readings,

41
00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.200
and they connect narratives
to their own lives.

42
00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:23.240
You know, the result is that the comment
section becomes a digital textual space

43
00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:28.080
where storytelling continues
long after the film itself has ended.

44
00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:33.720
Now, let me talk about my data
and the approach I did.

45
00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:38.640
To explore my argument, I conducted
a qualitative thematic analysis of

46
00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:42.720
audience comments drawn from

47
00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:44.200
two digital Nollywood texts.

48
00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:47.800
I looked at “Love in Every Word,”

49
00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:50.000
which was

50
00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.000
examined through comments
posted on YouTube.

51
00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:57.760
I looked at “Monica,” which was examined
through audience responses on Instagram.

52
00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:01.320
My interest was not in measuring
audiences’ behavior statistically.

53
00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:04.560
What I focused on
was how viewers construct

54
00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.800
meaning through their interactions
and how those interactions contribute

55
00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:11.400
to ongoing narrative production.

56
00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:15.480
Okay, so one of the strongest themes
that emerge from the data

57
00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:17.560
is empowerment and self-reliance.

58
00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:21.560
For example, you see one viewer says,
“Her skill has opened the door for her.”

59
00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:24.680
If you look at the slides,
you see there, another says, “They

60
00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:28.960
did not let some rich man come and save
her.” What is interesting here is that

61
00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.480
audiences are not simply describing
what happened in the film, you know,

62
00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:36.120
they are actively interpreting
the narrative and assigning ideological

63
00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:41.400
meaning to it rather than viewing Monica
as a story about romance or luck,

64
00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:44.920
you see that the viewers,
they reconstruct it as a story

65
00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:49.040
about female agency,
resilience, and economic independence.

66
00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:52.280
And in doing so, they extend the narrative
beyond the intentions

67
00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:55.720
of the filmmaker, and they participate
in the production of new meaning.

68
00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:57.680
A second

69
00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:00.880
pattern that involves—
is emotional identification.

70
00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:02.040
You see that several comments

71
00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:05.280
move beyond interpretation,
and they become personal testimony.

72
00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:09.480
One viewer shares her comment like, “This
is literally my story.” Another says,

73
00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:13.120
“I am a first daughter and I am tired.”
These comments are significant

74
00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:17.080
because they demonstrate how audiences
insert themselves into the narrative.

75
00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.880
The story becomes a framework,
you know, through which viewers

76
00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:23.960
articulate their own experiences,
their struggles, and their emotions.

77
00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:26.840
And at this point,
the comment section functions

78
00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:28.600
more than a space for discussion.

79
00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:31.320
It becomes a confessional
and affective space

80
00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:34.200
where storytelling merges
with lived experiences, you know,

81
00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:37.320
the narrative is no longer
only about fictional characters.

82
00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:41.560
It becomes intertwined with the realities
of the audience themselves.

83
00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:47.400
However, if we look at it, you see that
audience interpretation is not uniform.

84
00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:51.120
The comment section reveal competing
and sometimes conflicting readings.

85
00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:52.560
For example,

86
00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:53.560
many viewers

87
00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:56.920
celebrated the sacrifices made by
the female protagonists in these movies.

88
00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:00.240
But you see that there was a comment
that argued, “Stop hyping her.”

89
00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:04.040
This response challenges
the dominant interpretation and proposes

90
00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:06.840
and alternative reading of the same event.

91
00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:10.720
What we see here is that meaning
remains open and contested,

92
00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:10.960
you know,

93
00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:15.160
the comment section becomes a space
of negotiation where audiences continue—

94
00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:19.200
they debate what the narrative means
and what values it should represent.

95
00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:22.320
You know, it is precisely
this interpretative openness

96
00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:24.360
that makes these spaces

97
00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:28.600
so interesting from the perspective
of electronic literature.

98
00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:30.200
Now you may ask why?

99
00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:33.960
Why does this,
you know, matter to e-literature?

100
00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:38.080
I would like to

101
00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:41.360
state where I conclude by returning

102
00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:45.160
to the larger question that I asked
from the beginning of the study, you know,

103
00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:50.760
I said, if electronic literature
is concerned with born-digital,

104
00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:55.720
interactive,
and participatory forms of textuality,

105
00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:59.280
then we may need to broaden
our understanding

106
00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:02.840
of where electronic literary
practices can be found.

107
00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:07.320
You know, the audiences comments
examined in this study,

108
00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:10.920
they are not merely responsive
to a narrative, you know, they are

109
00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:16.280
collaborative acts of meaning making
through interpretation, disagreement,

110
00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:20.040
emotional testimony, and collective
reflection in the audiences.

111
00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:23.760
They extend stories beyond the screen,
and they transform

112
00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:26.960
comment sections
into dynamic digital textual spaces.

113
00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:32.760
So Digital Nollywood therefore invites us
to think about electronic literature

114
00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:35.880
not only as something created
by a single author,

115
00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:40.880
you know, but also as something
that emerges through participation,

116
00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:43.920
interaction, and co-creation.

117
00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:46.920
And in this sense,
the narrative does not end

118
00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:52.440
when the film ends, you know,
it continues to be written by an audience

119
00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:57.760
that has become a coauthoring community.

120
00:21:58.040 --> 00:21:59.520
Thank you very much.
